en greater concern frank's doctor  told barbara he could only treat her  husband  if he was in the home i called him i  begged his office  begged them she had no choice but to  accept the doctor's decision  but remain steadfast with hers it was  made easier by the fact that ontario  like several other provinces  determined residents who leave long-term  care homes can return to them  without going to the end of new wait  lists but there's a catch  the policy ends whenever the pandemic  does if ontario basically says as of  september 1st you know  we are now you know the pandemic is now  over as far  as far we're concerned you know then you  know after that period  then you know you're not protected under  these changes  ontario's ministry of long-term care  told global news in a statement  residents who choose to leave homes not  in outbreak  are discharged it's not what barbara  hoped to hear but  at least it's definitive much like her  resolve  it was a little bit scary but i knew it  would work out i just  had to be positive and do what i  i had to do what was best for him it's  her way of promising him true care  for the long term mike durley global  news toronto  competing without crowds next what an  olympics under a state of emergency  means for athletes  fans won't be a problem at the tokyo  olympics all spectators have been  banned as japan prepares for a fourth  state of emergency in the capital due to  covet 19.  now these games will be different from  any ever held before  and many wonder why they're still even  going ahead  crystal command seeing looks at how our  canadian athletes  are feeling  a warm welcome for british athletes  arriving in tokyo but their olympic  experience  will be like no other  [Music]  spectators will now be banned under new  emergency restrictions  as covet 19 infections here have reached  a two-month high  we'll support any measure  which is necessary to have safe  and secure olympic and paralympic  games until now officials 